{"name":"Workbench","hex":"#cda366","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"workbench","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sturdy bench or table at which manual work is done by a carpenter, machinist, etc.","A horizontal padded surface, usually adjustable in height and inclination and often with attached weight rack, used for proper posture during exercise."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/workbench"}],"entries":[{"title":"Workbench","blocks":[{"text":"A workbench is a sturdy table at which manual work is done. They range from simple flat surfaces to very complex designs that may be considered tools in themselves. Workbenches vary in size from tiny jewellers benches to the huge benches used by staircase makers. Almost all workbenches are rectangular in shape, often using the surface, corners and edges as flat/square and dimension standards. Design is as varied as the type of work for which the benches are used but most share these attributes:"},{"items":["A comfortable height for working with provisions for seated or standing work","A way to fix the workpiece to the surface so that it may be worked with both hands","Provisions for mounting, storing and accessing tools"]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Carpenter%27s_workbench.jpg/330px-Carpenter%27s_workbench.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workbench","wikidata":"Q347690"},{"title":"Workbench (AmigaOS)","blocks":[{"text":"Workbench is the desktop environment and graphical file manager of AmigaOS developed by Commodore International for their Amiga line of computers. Workbench provides the user with a graphical interface to work with file systems and launch applications. It uses a workbench metaphor for representing file system organisation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/3/3f/AmigaOS_4.1_Update_2.png/330px-AmigaOS_4.1_Update_2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workbench_(AmigaOS)","wikidata":"Q336405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png/500px-A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png/1280px-A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png/500px-A-J_Roubo_-_L%27Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Original Roubo workbench plans, 1769","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA-J_Roubo_-_L'Art_du_Menuisier_-_Planche_011.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Electronics_workbench.jpg/500px-Electronics_workbench.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Electronics_workbench.jpg/1280px-Electronics_workbench.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Electronics_workbench.jpg/500px-Electronics_workbench.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The workbench of a hobby electronics enthusiast with an oscilloscope, PC, two digital multimeters, soldering station, pocket calculator, small tools, a stabilized power supply unit and storage boxes on the wall for electronic components","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElectronics_workbench.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d